.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAD-PURGE-EVENTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ead-purge-events \- remove audit entries older than a given age
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ead purge-events
.BR \-\-older\-than\-days | \-d
.I N
.SH DESCRIPTION
Removes audit entries older than the given age.
.SH RETENTION IS NORMALLY NOT THIS
Each entry is written with an expiry taken from
.IR euclid.modules.ead.retention ,
and the database removes it when it comes. Nothing has to be run for that
to happen, and nothing has to be scheduled.
.PP
This command is for the installation that changed its mind: a retention
that was shortened after years had already accumulated, or a window that
has to be gone now rather than when its entries expire.
.SH AN AGE IS REQUIRED
.B \-\-older\-than\-days
must be given and must be positive. A purge with no window would remove
the whole trail, and that is not something to do by leaving an argument
out.
.SH IT IS ITSELF AUDITED
The purge is recorded like any other command that changes something, so
the trail says who pruned it and by how much. That entry is newer than
anything the purge removed, so it survives its own command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-older\-than\-days " " \fIN\fR
Remove entries created more than this many days ago. Required, and must
be positive.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.RI ( "{removed}" )
is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
No age was given, the age was not positive, the caller was not
authenticated, or the caller does not hold
.IR ead:purge-events " - which only " account-administrator
holds, since removing audit records is not an operator's job.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Keep the last ninety days
euclid-cli ead purge-events --older-than-days 90

# See what it would be working on first
euclid-cli ead count-events
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ead (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ead-count-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ead-list-events (1)
